erbury synagogue building fund, with the congregation’s thanks. About £200 was still needed; the land had been bought and the committee hoped to lay the first stone shortly. Subscriptions were received by Messrs. Moses and Levy, Aldgate; Mr. Isaac Lyons, Duke-street, Aldgate; the editors of the Jewish Chronicle and the Voice of Jacob; and Mr. J. Jacobs, 29 Palace-street, Canterbury, secretary to the committee. A deputation was to visit London to solicit subscriptions. 74 entries totalling £404 1s. 6d. as transcribed; no total is printed. Places are given only where printed."}, {"id": "edelman-relief-1847", "title": "Appeal of Hirsch Edelman, printer and publisher of Königsberg: donations already received", "title_original": "Donations already received", "list_type": "donation", "source_id": "jewish-chronicle", "date": "1847-09-03", "citation": "Vol. 3, no. 26", "pages": "p. 235", "purpose": "Support for Hirsch Edelman, whose Hebrew publications for the Jews of Russia were left on his hands by the Russian ukase restricting the import of Hebrew books.", "organizer_id": "", "place_id": "london", "beneficiary_place_id": "konigsberg", "currency": "GBP", "image": "/images/jewish-chronicle-1847-09-03-p235-edelman.jpg", "image_credit": "Internet Archive (from microfilm)", "iiif_manifest": "", "status": "full transcription — not yet checked", "transcriber": "Claude (draft)", "featured": "no", "notes": "Printed under the heading “Lost, on Tuesday last, an appeal, of which the following is a copy”; whoever found it was asked to bring it to 28 Bury-street, St. Mary-Axe. The appeal is signed Hirsch Edelman, printer and publisher, of Königsberg, September 5607, and cites the Chief Rabbi, Dr. Adler, as able to confirm his statements. A note says the date of the document had been altered from July to September “to prevent any imposition being practised on the benevolent”. Nine donations totalling £11 15s. 6d. as transcribed; no total is printed."}], "places": [{"id": "nottingham", 